/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch30_fast_equilibrium_full -- a fast reversible step feeding a slow
  one, every rate integrated:

      A  <-->  B      kf = 0.1 1/s, kr = 0.025 1/s   (K = kf/kr = 4, fast)
      B  --->  C      k  = 0.001 1/s                 (slow)

  Isothermal at 350 K, first order throughout, Ea = 0 so each rate constant
  is exactly the number written in constant/reactions.

  THE QUESTION.  A and B interconvert a hundred times faster than B leaves
  for C.  After a short first moment they sit at equilibrium with each
  other, n_B = K n_A, and the pair behaves as ONE pool that drains into C
  at the rate of the B it contains:

      d(n_A + n_B)/dt = -k n_B = -k K/(1+K) (n_A + n_B)

  That is partial equilibrium, and batch31_fast_equilibrium_reduced runs
  exactly that law, on one pool, with no kf and no kr.  The two answers
  for n_C differ by the delay of the first moment, while B is still
  forming: measured with these numbers the largest gap is 0.62 % of the
  charge, and it falls as kf/k grows.  The EduTool page "When the fast
  obeys the slow" changes kf and kr together (K held at 4) and draws the
  gap.

  THE COST.  The fast pair sets a rate constant of kf + kr = 0.125 1/s.  An
  explicit fixed RK4 step would have to stay below 2.785/0.125 = 22 s, and
  below 0.0028 s at the page's largest ratio; the adaptive Rosenbrock23
  integrator declared in controlDict prints every step it takes.

  No species carries formation data and none is needed: isothermal mode
  holds T, so the heat of reaction is not used (the run says so), the same
  posture as batch03_consecutive.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        batchReactor;

        operation
        {
            mode         isothermal;
            T_setpoint   350.0 K;
        }

        reactions  ( A_to_B  B_to_A  B_to_C );
    }
);
